module j3.text
    `string` is a compiler lang item (read-only `[]u8`). Mutate via Text.
    `cstr` never appears in pub game APIs.
    Any thread: the `cstr` scratch and the `parseF64` fallback buffer are per thread (`j3c doc threads`).
    Strings: `startsWith` `endsWith` `trim` `find` `lastIndexOf` `replace`, cursors `split(s, sep)`
    and `lines(s)` (iterators: `for part in split(s, ",") { }`). UTF-8: `decodeRune(s, i): (u32, int)`,
    `isValidUtf8`, `encodeRune`, `runes(s)`. Numbers: `parseInt(s, base)` / `parseU64` /
    `parseF64` return `Option` (overflow is None; floats are correctly rounded), and
    `writeF64` / `Text.pushF64` / `pushF32` print the shortest text that reads back to the same bits.
    Formatting into a `Text`: `fmtInt` (thousands), `fmtBytes` (file sizes), `fmtTimeUtc` (RFC 3339 from `sys.unixMs`).

fn cstr(s: string): cstr
    Copy `s` into a fixed scratch buffer and return a NUL-terminated C string.
fn scratchText(): Text
    An empty `Text` on the frame scratch arena (`j3.memory.scratch`; `App` rewinds it at the start of every frame).
    Build a string in a markup function and return `t.str()`; the view is valid until the next frame starts.
struct Text
    Growable UTF-8 byte buffer allocated from an arena.
    bytes: []u8
        Live bytes (`len` is the string length; capacity is `cap`).
    cap: int
        Allocated capacity in bytes.
    arena: &Arena
        Arena used for growth.
    fn Text.new(arena: &Arena): Text
        Empty text buffer on `arena`.
    fn Text.push(this: &Text, b: u8)
        Append one byte, growing capacity from 0→16 then ×2 as needed.
    fn Text.pushStr(this: &Text, s: string)
        Append every byte of `s`.
    fn Text.scratch(): &Text
        An empty `Text` on the frame scratch arena (see `scratchText`), for a chain of `add`.
        Generated `.j3ui` text with `{...}` holes is `Text.scratch().add("n = ").add(`{n}`).str()`.
    fn Text.add(this: &Text, s: string): &Text
        Append `s` and return this `Text`, so appends chain.
    fn Text.pushBytes(this: &Text, b: []u8)
        Append every byte of `b`, growing once.
    fn Text.clear(this: &Text)
        Empty the buffer, keeping its capacity.
    fn Text.len(this: &Text): int
        Current byte length.
    fn Text.insert(this: &Text, at: int, s: string)
        Insert `s` before byte `at` (clamped to the text).
    fn Text.remove(this: &Text, at: int, n: int)
        Remove `n` bytes starting at `at` (clamped to the text).
    fn Text.pop(this: &Text)
        Drop the last byte. No change when the buffer is empty.
    fn Text.str(this: &Text): string
        Bytes currently stored, as a `string` view.
    fn Text.pushInt(this: &Text, n: int)
        Append decimal digits of `n` (with a leading `-` when negative).
    fn Text.pushFloat(this: &Text, value: f32)
        Append a decimal f32 rounded to six fractional digits, trailing zeros trimmed (`1.5`, `2.0`).
    fn Text.fmt(this: &Text, s: string): &Text
        Append `s` and return this `Text`. With a backtick string, `t.fmt(`n = {n}`)`, the compiler
        appends each part straight into `t`: no 512-byte interpolation limit, no intermediate string.
    fn Text.pushLine(this: &Text, s: string)
        Append `s` and a newline.
    fn Text.indent(this: &Text, n: int)
        Append `n` spaces.
    fn Text.pushU64(this: &Text, v: u64)
        Append the decimal digits of `v`, unsigned (`18446744073709551615`).
    fn Text.pushHex(this: &Text, v: u64)
        Append `v` in lowercase hex, no `0x` and no padding (`ff`, `0`).
    fn Text.pushF64(this: &Text, v: f64)
        Append `v` as the fewest digits that read back as exactly `v` (`writeF64`: `0.1`, `1e+300`,
        `5e-324`, `inf`, `nan`); `parseF64` gives the same bits back.
    fn Text.pushF32(this: &Text, v: f32)
        Append `v` as the fewest digits that read back as exactly this f32 (`writeF32`: `0.1` for
        `f32(0.1)`). `pushFloat` is the fixed six-decimal form.
    fn Text.pushChar(this: &Text, cp: u32)
        Append code point `cp` as UTF-8; a surrogate or a value past U+10FFFF appends U+FFFD.
    fn Text.flushTo(this: &Text, fd: int): bool
        Write every byte to file descriptor `fd` and empty the buffer, keeping its capacity, so a
        big output (generated code) can be written in pieces: `if t.len() > 65536 { t.flushTo(fd); }`.
        False when a write fails; the unwritten bytes stay in the buffer.

fn fmtInt(t: &Text, n: int): string
    Append `n` with a comma between thousands (`1,234,567`, `-42`) to `t` and return just what was appended,
    a view into `t` (valid until `t` grows or is cleared; the caller owns `t`). Every i64 works.
fn fmtBytes(t: &Text, n: int): string
    Append a file size to `t` (`0 B`, `999 B`, `1.0 KB`, `1.5 MB`, `2.0 GB`; TB, PB, EB follow) and return
    just what was appended, a view into `t` like `fmtInt`. Units are 1024 (a KB is 1024 bytes, as the desk-app
    file list showed), one decimal below the unit, truncated (1023.9 KB never rounds up to `1024.0 KB`).
    Negative sizes get a leading `-`.
fn fmtTimeUtc(t: &Text, ms: int): string
    Append `ms` (Unix milliseconds, `sys.unixMs()`) as RFC 3339 UTC with milliseconds (`2026-11-19T21:04:05.123Z`)
    to `t` and return just what was appended, a view into `t` like `fmtInt`. Before 1970 works; years past 9999
    print more digits.
fn same(a: string, b: string): bool
    Byte-wise equality of two strings (length then contents).
fn find(s: string, part: string): int
    Byte index of the first `part` in `s`, or -1. An empty `part` is found at 0.
fn contains(s: string, part: string): bool
    Whether `part` appears in `s`.
fn cstrBytes(s: []u8): cstr
    Copy a byte slice into scratch and return a NUL-terminated C string.
fn parseInt(s: string, base: int): Option<int>
    `s` as an `int` in `base` (2..36; another base panics). An optional `+` or `-` comes first, then
    one or more digits (`a`-`z` either case for 10-35). No `0x`/`0b` prefix, `_`, or spaces: pass
    the base and strip those yourself. None for an empty string, a lone sign, a bad digit, or a
    value outside the i64 range (`-9223372036854775808` reads).
fn parseU64(s: string, base: int): Option<u64>
    `s` as a `u64` in `base` (2..36): digits only, no sign, prefix, `_`, or spaces. None for an
    empty string, a bad digit, or a value past 18446744073709551615.
fn parseF64(s: string): Option<f64>
    `s` as the f64 nearest to its exact decimal value (ties to even), so `writeF64` output reads
    back to the same bits. Grammar: optional `+`/`-`, then digits with at most one `.` (at least
    one digit, `.5` and `5.` read), then optionally `e`/`E`, a sign, and one or more digits; or
    `inf`, `infinity`, `nan` in any case. None for anything else (spaces, `0x`, `_`, an empty
    string). Too big gives an infinity and too small a zero, both signed, as C's `strtod` does.
fn f64Bits(x: f64): u64
    The IEEE bits of `x`.
fn f64FromBits(b: u64): f64
    The f64 with IEEE bits `b`.
fn f32Bits(x: f32): u32
    The IEEE bits of `x`.
fn f32FromBits(b: u32): f32
    The f32 with IEEE bits `b`.
fn writeF64(out: []u8, x: f64): int
    Write `x` into `out` (at least 32 bytes) as the fewest significant digits that read back as
    exactly `x` (closest to `x` when several have that many), laid out like C's `%g`: `0.1`,
    `123.456`, `1e+300`, `5e-324`, `-0`, `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`. Returns the length.
    `parseF64` reads every output back to the same bits (except NaN payloads).
fn writeF32(out: []u8, x: f32): int
    `writeF64` for an f32: the fewest digits that read back as this f32 (`0.1` for `f32(0.1)`,
    where `writeF64(f64(x))` gives `0.10000000149011612`).
fn startsWith(s: string, prefix: string): bool
    Whether `s` begins with `prefix` (always for an empty one).
fn endsWith(s: string, suffix: string): bool
    Whether `s` ends with `suffix` (always for an empty one).
fn isSpace(c: u8): bool
    ASCII whitespace: space, tab, newline, vertical tab, form feed, carriage return.
fn trimStart(s: string): string
    `s` without ASCII whitespace at the start.
fn trimEnd(s: string): string
    `s` without ASCII whitespace at the end.
fn trim(s: string): string
    `s` without ASCII whitespace at either end.
fn lastIndexOf(s: string, part: string): int
    Byte index of the last `part` in `s`, or -1. An empty `part` is found at `s.len`.
fn replace(t: &Text, s: string, old: string, new: string): string
    Append `s` with every `old` replaced by `new` (left to right, not overlapping) to `t`, and return
    what was appended, a view into `t` like `fmtInt`. An empty `old` matches nothing.
struct Split
    The pieces of `s` between each `sep`, as an iterator:
    for part in text.split("a,,b", ",") { use(part); }     // "a", "", "b"
    Every separator splits, so `n` separators give `n + 1` pieces: "" gives one empty piece, ",a,"
    gives "", "a", "". An empty `sep` panics.
    s: string
    sep: string
    at: int
    done: bool
    fn Split.next(this: &Split): Option<string>
        The next piece, or None when there is none.

fn split(s: string, sep: string): Split
    A `Split` cursor over `s` (see `Split`).
struct Lines
    The lines of `s`, each without its `\n` (or `\r\n`), as an iterator: `for line in lines(s) { }`.
    A final line break ends the last line instead of starting an empty one: "a\nb\n" and "a\nb"
    both give "a", "b"; "" gives none; "\n" gives one empty line.
    s: string
    at: int
    fn Lines.next(this: &Lines): Option<string>
        The next line, or None at the end.

fn lines(s: string): Lines
    A `Lines` cursor over `s`.
fn decodeRune(s: string, i: int): (u32, int)
    The code point starting at byte `i` of `s` (`i` below `s.len`) and its length in bytes. Bytes
    that are not valid UTF-8 give U+FFFD with length 1, so each bad byte becomes one U+FFFD and
    decoding goes on at the next byte (a sequence cut off by the end of `s` too).
fn encodeRune(t: &Text, cp: u32)
    Append `cp` as UTF-8 to `t` (the same as `t.pushChar(cp)`): a surrogate or a value past
    U+10FFFF appends U+FFFD.
fn isValidUtf8(s: string): bool
    Whether all of `s` is valid UTF-8 (an encoded U+FFFD counts as valid).
struct Runes
    The code points of a string, an iterator:
    for cp in s.runes() { use(cp); }
    let r = s.runes();
    for cp in &r { use(cp, r.at); }    // r.at is the byte where cp starts
    Bad bytes come out as U+FFFD, one per byte (see `decodeRune`).
    cp: u32
        The last code point `next` gave.
    at: int
        Byte offset of `cp` in the string.
    s: string
    pos: int
    fn Runes.next(this: &Runes): Option<u32>
        The next code point; `None` at the end.

fn runes(s: string): Runes
    A `Runes` cursor over `s`; with `use j3.text.runes;` also `s.runes()`.
